+#### Offcanvas
+
+The `Offcanvas` class owns one offcanvas panel, the element Bootstrap's markup marks with the `host`
+token, and slides it in from its edge over the cascade's backdrop in the page's flow: the panel and
+its backdrop keep the cascade's `z-index` rungs and never enter the top layer. Construction
+validates and copies the `classes`, `attributes`, and `selectors` groups, resolves the options,
+claims the host, binds the hooks, and listens on the host for Escape and on the window for a
+resize. It writes nothing. The panel validates its `selectors` group and
+keeps none of it: the delegate routes offcanvas clicks by the selectors, and a panel you construct
+directly matches with none.
+
+Showing takes a reference to the document's scroll lock when the `scroll` option is `false`, and,
+when the `backdrop` option is `true`, shows the backdrop in the host's parent, where it fades in
+beside the slide. It then writes `aria-modal="true"` and `role="dialog"`, forces a layout read, adds
+the `showing` and `shown` tokens, and waits for the panel's transition and the backdrop's fade to
+settle. It removes the `showing` token and, when the `backdrop` option is `true` or the `scroll`
+option is `false`, isolates the rest of the document and moves focus to the host. It then
+dispatches `shown.vn.offcanvas`. Hiding releases the isolation, adds the `hiding` token, starts the
+backdrop's fade out, removes the `shown` token, and waits for the panel's transition and the fade to
+settle. It then removes the `hiding` token, and a `showing` token a stopped show left behind,
+removes `aria-modal` and `role`, removes the backdrop as a step of its own, releases the scroll lock,
+and dispatches `hidden.vn.offcanvas`. The `show`, `hide`, and `toggle` methods resolve `true` after the completed
+event. Each resolves `false` when the panel already carries the state, a change is in flight, a
+listener prevented the pre-change event, the panel is destroyed, or another write changed the host's
+`shown` token at a door the call read before it dispatched its completed event. A listener to the
+completed event that hides or shows the panel starts a change of its own, so a hide a
+`shown.vn.offcanvas` hook completes leaves the show's `true` in place. The `shown` property reads
+the `shown` token on every access.
+
+The shipped cascade slides the panel through a `transform` transition of `0.3s`, and the fade
+partial fades the backdrop through its `.fade` rule, so each wait lasts as long as the longer of the
+two. Under reduced motion the cascade writes `transition: none` on both, and each call completes in
+a microtask.
+
+Escape on the host hides the panel when `dismiss.escape` is `true`, and a press on the backdrop hides
+it when `dismiss.backdrop` is `true`. When `dismiss.escape` is `false`, Escape on the shown panel
+instead dispatches `prevent.vn.offcanvas`, and so does a press on the backdrop when
+`dismiss.backdrop` is `false`; the panel writes no token for either refusal. The panel listens for
+`mousedown` on the backdrop element it creates, as Bootstrap's `Backdrop` utility does, and the
+isolation spares the backdrop, so a press beside the panel lands on the backdrop itself. A press on
+any other element, a container of the backdrop included, counts for nothing.
+
+A window resize hides a shown panel whose computed `position` is no longer `fixed`, subject to the
+prevention of its `hide.vn.offcanvas` event. A responsive panel, one carrying a class such as
+`offcanvas-lg`, is fixed below its breakpoint and sits in the flow at and above it, as § Offcanvas
+classes states, so a resize past the breakpoint hides it. A bare `offcanvas` panel is fixed at every
+width, so no resize hides it. Each panel listens for its own lifetime and reads its own host. A
+resize that arrives while a show is in flight is applied after that show dispatches
+`shown.vn.offcanvas`, from the position the host then computes.
+
+The panel composes the `ScrollLock`, `Backdrop`, and `Isolation` classes the Modal subsection
+describes. It passes the scroll lock no selectors, so the lock compensates the
+`SCROLL_LOCK_SELECTORS` defaults, as Bootstrap's offcanvas does. It passes the backdrop its own
+`backdrop`, `shown`, and `fade` tokens and the host's parent, and the backdrop always fades. It
+passes the isolation the trigger its `show` call received and the backdrop element as the one
+element to spare. It passes its own lifetime as the scroll lock's and the isolation's signal.
+
+The panel writes and tests the following class tokens, under the names its `classes` group
+resolves; each default is the token the shipped cascade selects on.
+
+| Key        | Default              | Marks                                                                 |
+| ---------- | -------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------- |
+| `host`     | `offcanvas`          | The panel, which a dismiss trigger naming no target hides from inside |
+| `shown`    | `show`               | A shown panel, and its shown backdrop                                 |
+| `showing`  | `showing`            | The panel while it slides in                                          |
+| `hiding`   | `hiding`             | The panel while it slides out                                         |
+| `backdrop` | `offcanvas-backdrop` | The panel's backdrop element                                          |
+| `fade`     | `fade`               | The panel's backdrop, which always fades                              |
+| `disabled` | `disabled`           | A trigger or a dismiss trigger that neither shows nor hides the panel |
+
+The panel and its triggers read the following attributes, under the names the `attributes` group
+resolves; each default is Bootstrap's attribute name. The `backdrop` attribute feeds two option
+paths: `backdrop`, which only `false` or `0` turns off, and `dismiss.backdrop`, which only `static`
+turns off.
+
+| Key        | Default            | Feeds                                                                                  | Read through                                         |
+| ---------- | ------------------ | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------- |
+| `target`   | `data-bs-target`   | The panel a trigger names, before its `href` fragment                                  | The `readTarget` function                            |
+| `backdrop` | `data-bs-backdrop` | The `backdrop` and `dismiss.backdrop` options, each unless the constructor supplies it | The `parseBackdrop` function                         |
+| `escape`   | `data-bs-keyboard` | The `dismiss.escape` option, unless the constructor supplies it                        | The `parseBoolean` function of `@orkestrel/contract` |
+| `scroll`   | `data-bs-scroll`   | The `scroll` option, unless the constructor supplies it                                | The `parseBoolean` function of `@orkestrel/contract` |
+
+The delegate matches offcanvas triggers with the following selectors, under the names the
+`selectors` group resolves; each default is Bootstrap's selector.
+
+| Key       | Default                         | Selects                                             |
+| --------- | ------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------- |
+| `trigger` | `[data-bs-toggle="offcanvas"]`  | The triggers a delegated click toggles a panel from |
+| `dismiss` | `[data-bs-dismiss="offcanvas"]` | The triggers a delegated click hides a panel from   |
+
+The `OFFCANVAS_DEFAULTS` constant holds the option values a panel takes when neither its attributes
+nor its constructor supply them, which are Bootstrap's defaults.
+
+| Option             | Default | Decides                                                          |
+| ------------------ | ------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------- |
+| `backdrop`         | `true`  | Whether the backdrop shows behind the panel                      |
+| `dismiss.backdrop` | `true`  | Whether a press on the backdrop hides the panel                  |
+| `dismiss.escape`   | `true`  | Whether Escape hides the panel                                   |
+| `scroll`           | `false` | Whether document scrolling stays available while the panel shows |
+
+The panel dispatches the following events on its host. Each one bubbles and carries a detail object
+whose `relatedTarget` member names the trigger, as the following table states; the
+`isOffcanvasEvent` function checks that shape.
+
+| Key       | Wire name              | Cancelable | Detail `relatedTarget`       | Dispatched                                                                      |
+| --------- | ---------------------- | ---------- | ---------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
+| `show`    | `show.vn.offcanvas`    | Yes        | The trigger passed to `show` | Before any write; prevention refuses the show                                   |
+| `shown`   | `shown.vn.offcanvas`   | No         | The trigger passed to `show` | After the slide and the backdrop's fade settle and focus moves                  |
+| `hide`    | `hide.vn.offcanvas`    | Yes        | Undefined                    | Before any write; prevention refuses the hide                                   |
+| `hidden`  | `hidden.vn.offcanvas`  | No         | Undefined                    | After the backdrop leaves and the scroll lock is released                       |
+| `prevent` | `prevent.vn.offcanvas` | No         | Undefined                    | When Escape or a press on the backdrop meets a dismissal option that is `false` |
+
+A call reads the host at each of its doors for the phase it is in: until a show adds the `shown`
+token, and from the moment a hide removes it, the host must lack the token, and in between it must
+carry it. A call that finds the host otherwise or the panel destroyed reads the change as taken
+over: it stops, writing and dispatching nothing more, and resolves `false`. The doors end when the
+call dispatches its completed event: a listener to that event that shows or hides the panel starts
+a new change, and the call whose event it heard still resolves `true`. Every other `show` or `hide`
+call is refused while a change is in flight, so a listener that shows the panel again inside
+`show.vn.offcanvas` runs the one show and dispatches the one `shown.vn.offcanvas` event: its own
+call resolves `true`, and the call whose event it heard resolves `false`. A call that only the host
+took over also ends the change in flight. What that call acquired, such as the scroll lock or the
+backdrop, stays held until one of the following exits releases it: a later `show` call, which takes
+the held resources over, and the `hide` call after it; a `hide` call made after the host carries
+the `shown` token again; or destruction. A `hide` call while the host lacks the token resolves
+`false` and releases nothing. A hide the host took over removes no backdrop: the backdrop's fade
+writes nothing past its settle, and the hide removes the element only as a step after its own door
+reads. The backdrop is a plain `div` the panel creates, so no reaction runs inside its writes and no
+door follows them. The scroll lock and the isolation each read the panel's
+lifetime as their signal while they are built, so a destruction a reaction runs inside either
+construction stops it and releases what it took, and a lock or an isolation whose construction
+another write took the show over from is released rather than kept. Destruction aborts the wait,
+releases the isolation, the scroll lock, and the Escape and resize listeners, removes the backdrop
+with its press listener, and restores the host's `shown`, `showing`, and `hiding` tokens and its
+`aria-modal` and `role` attributes.
+
+The delegate's `offcanvas` option carries the panel's groups. A click inside a trigger the resolved
+`trigger` selector matches toggles the panel the trigger's target attribute or `href` fragment names
+inside the root, passing the trigger to `toggle`, through the panel the static `find` method returns
+or one the delegate acquires with the group. The toggle route prevents an anchor's or an area's default
+action first and then toggles nothing when the `matchesDisabled` function reads the trigger as
+disabled with the `disabled` token, as Bootstrap's data API does. Before the toggle it hides the
+first shown panel inside the root, the first element carrying the `host` and `shown` tokens, when
+that element is not the named panel, through the panel `find` returns for it. It arms the focus
+return before it calls `toggle` and disarms it when the toggle resolves `false`: the panel's next
+`hidden.vn.offcanvas` event returns focus to the trigger, which the platform refuses while the
+trigger is disconnected or not rendered. A click inside a trigger the `dismiss` selector matches
+hides the panel its target attribute or `href` fragment names inside the root, or else the closest
+element around it carrying the `host` token, through the panel `find` returns or one the delegate
+acquires, by the dismiss resolution the alert, modal, and toast dismiss routes share. The offcanvas
+routes run after the toast route, the toggle route before the dismiss route. A delegate that a
+listener earlier in the click destroyed prevents, marks, and drives nothing through the toggle
+route, and one that a listener to the other panel's `hide.vn.offcanvas` event destroys marks and
+toggles nothing, which leaves the named panel to a live outer delegate. Each route leaves a panel
+outside the root alone, and under nested roots each panel is driven once per click. A click is
+refused before any route runs, as § Delegation states, when a panel its enabled offcanvas trigger
+names, or its enabled offcanvas dismiss trigger reaches, is also another route's constructing host,
+such as its button host or one of the panels its collapse trigger names, and neither engine exists
+on that element. A panel both offcanvas routes reach on one click counts once, and the dismiss route
+then leaves it to the toggle route.
+
+Neither Bootstrap's installed offcanvas nor the engine hides a shown modal when a panel shows; the
+modal's own data API hides only another shown modal. The panel departs from Bootstrap's in these
+ways:
+
+- The engine adds the `shown` token with the `showing` token before the slide in, and removes it
+  before the slide out, because that token is the panel's shown state and every door reads it. The
+  panel therefore carries `show showing` while it slides in and `hiding` alone while it slides out.
+  Bootstrap's panel carries `showing` alone while it slides in and `show hiding` while it slides
+  out, and a style or a query on `.offcanvas.show` sees the two timelines apart.
+
+- The events carry the `.vn.` namespace, and the trigger travels in `detail.relatedTarget`, as
+  § Events states. The `prevent` event replaces `hidePrevented`, and the `shown`, `hidden`, and
+  `prevent` events are not cancelable. Bootstrap's `EventHandler` makes every one of them cancelable.
+- A call while a change is in flight resolves `false`. Bootstrap's `hide` method clears its shown
+  flag before the slide out, so a `show` call during the slide out runs, and a `hide` call during
+  the slide in runs as well.
+- Hiding calls no `blur` on the panel, where Bootstrap's `hide` method does. A panel that isolates
+  moves focus to the trigger instead, as it releases the isolation.
+- The engine adopts no `.offcanvas.show` markup at the window's `load` event, where Bootstrap's data
+  API calls `show` on each such panel. You construct the engine over shown markup instead, and its
+  `shown` property reads the token, so its `hide` method hides the panel.
+- The resize hide is per panel. Bootstrap's resize handler queries the document for
+  `[aria-modal][class*=show][class*=offcanvas-]` and requests `hide` on each match whose computed
+  position is not `fixed`, which hides only a panel Bootstrap itself showed, and its query also
+  matches the `showing` class, so the request lands during the slide in. The engine hides every live
+  panel whose host carries its `shown` token, whatever its other classes, and applies a resize during
+  the slide in after the slide in settles.
+- Focus stays inside the panel because everything outside it but its backdrop is inert, which
+  also keeps the pointer and assistive technology out. Bootstrap's `FocusTrap` utility listens for `focusin` on the
+  document and moves focus back into the panel.
+- Everything outside the panel except its backdrop is inert, so a press on an element painted above
+  the backdrop, such as a toast container or a menu, falls through to the backdrop and dismisses
+  the panel. Under Bootstrap's `FocusTrap` that element stays interactive and receives the press
+  itself.
+- Each completed event waits for the backdrop's fade as well as the panel's slide. Bootstrap's waits
+  on the panel's transition alone and leaves the backdrop's fade to finish on its own.
+- Completion waits on the running finite animations, with no timer. Bootstrap waits on a
+  `transitionend` listener with a fallback timer.
+- The scroll lock is shared by reference count with the modal's and every other panel's, so another
+  shown panel or modal keeps the page locked until the last one hides. Each Bootstrap panel's hide
+  resets the scrollbar compensation.
+- The backdrop is created at each show and destroyed after each hide. Bootstrap creates one backdrop
+  per panel at construction.
+- The `data-bs-backdrop` attribute is read as the modal reads it, and the modal's departures state
+  how Bootstrap's normalization of it differs. The `data-bs-keyboard` and `data-bs-scroll`
+  attributes accept `true`, `false`, `1`, and `0`, where Bootstrap's normalization turns `1` and `0`
+  into numbers its boolean type check refuses.
+- The toggle route hides the first shown panel inside the root that is not the named one, through
+  the panel `find` returns for it. Bootstrap's data API hides the first `.offcanvas.show` element in
+  the document, and fails when that element has no instance; the delegate leaves such an element
+  alone.
+- The toggle route arms its focus return before the toggle and disarms it when the toggle resolves
+  `false`. Bootstrap's data API leaves its `hidden.bs.offcanvas` listener armed until the panel next
+  hides.
+- Destruction abandons a transition in flight, removes the backdrop, and restores the panel,
+  scrolling, and focus. Bootstrap's `dispose` method removes the backdrop and deactivates its focus
+  trap, and restores nothing.
+
